 \section{Conclusion}
 
 We developed and evaluated a different agents based on Double DQN, a dueling network architecture, engineered spatial and scalar features, action masking, and a staged training curriculum. 
-The curriculum successfully decomposed the task into progressively harder objectives: the agent learned coin collection perfectly in \texttt{coin-heaven} and acquired effective crate-destruction behavior in \texttt{loot-crate}. 
-In the competitive stages, it also learned to destroy crates, collect coins, and occasionally eliminate opponents while keeping self-destruction rates comparatively low.
-
-In the case of the \texttt{ruehl\_base\_agent}, the agent struggled to achieve consistent success against hard opponents, highlighting the gap between performance in simplified training environments and competitive \texttt{classic} games.
-In particular, the best model against hard opponents achieved a mean score of $3.95$ over 20 evaluation games, with a mean of $0.40$ opponent eliminations and no completed rounds.  
-Thus, the agent learned meaningful bomb-use and survival-related behavior, but is unable to overcome the enemies counter-strategies consistently.
+The curriculum decomposed the task into progressively harder objectives. The reported selected \texttt{coin-heaven} summary collected all 50 coins, and the \texttt{loot-crate} summary recorded substantial crate destruction; these results describe the corresponding selection batches rather than guaranteeing perfect generalization. 
+The historical RUEHL selection summaries report crate destruction, coin collection, credited eliminations, and self-destruction fractions of 5--20\% in the competitive stages. These selected batches are distinct from the fresh frozen-policy experiment.
+
+The reported selected hard-opponent RUEHL summary gives mean score $3.95$, $0.40$ credited eliminations, and zero completion over a configured twenty-game selection batch. These checkpoint-selection statistics are distinct from the independent component evaluation.  
+These summaries show credited eliminations, but do not establish a competitive win rate or explain particular opponent counter-strategies. Zero completion under the strict all-coins criterion must not be interpreted as zero survival.
 
 Possible next steps include evaluation over longer training periods, more systematic hyperparameter tuning, and training against a diverse population of opponents. These extensions would clarify which design choices contribute most strongly to performance and may improve robustness in the full competitive setting.
 
-The other approach we took with the \texttt{final\_agent} showed that a more complex network and a more restrictive action mask and reward shaping can lead to a faster convergence of the network when only looking at the amount of games played. However, due to the significantly longer training time per game, the final agent was not able to achieve the same level of performance in the same amount of time as the \texttt{ruehl\_based\_agent}. This suggests that the more complex network and the more restrictive action mask and reward shaping are not necessarily better than a simpler approach, but rather a different approach to the same problem, given different constraints.\\
-It can be said that for the task at hand with the given time constraints and the computational resources that we had, the simpler approach of the \texttt{ruehl\_based\_agent} was more effective than the more complex approach of the \texttt{final\_agent}. However, it is possible that with more time and resources, the \texttt{final\_agent} could surpass the performance of the \texttt{ruehl\_based\_agent}.
-
-Possible next steps or improvements, to definetly determine the effectiveness of the \texttt{final\_agent} would be, to train it for a longer period of time, or use a more powerful computer with a gpu to speed up the training time per game.
+The final-agent run shows improvement in the simpler loot-crate stage and limited attack success in the short classic stage. Because training histories and measurement protocols differ, this does not establish faster convergence, inferior architecture, or a common-budget advantage for either design. RUEHL was the submitted artifact, while the final agent provides a contrasting allocation of learned decisions and explicit guidance.\\
+The controlled frozen-policy campaign measures the two masks with one verified final-agent checkpoint under common opponents and seeds. With features and weights held fixed, it identifies how the complete acting mask and learned action ranking contribute to score and survival for the final policy. Comparable retraining budgets and independent evaluation remain necessary to distinguish training exposure from changes in architecture or guidance.
 
 
